<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont Cenis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Col du Mont Cenis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont Cenis is a pass at about 2,080 metres in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> valley in Savoy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. It was one of the main routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, controlled by the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and later the house of Savoy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> built a road over it between 1803 and 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-cenis.jpg" alt="Mont Cenis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunther Hissler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Col_du_Mont_Cenis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
